Strict alignment: test bases once for their accesses in several blocks (applies after perf-80).

perf-80 tests a base once for a run of accesses inside one block, so a base read between the branches
of a carry chain or a compare is still tested in every block: the limbs of a UInt256 passed by `in`,
the fields of a struct passed by reference. UInt256.AddMod and ReduceSumAssumingLT2m kept 16 tests of
their parameters on the hot path, one per block and parameter.

Lowering now first versions regions of blocks (TryVersionAlignmentRegion): an entry block that
accesses a base and the blocks only the region leads to. One test at the start of the entry covers
that base and every other base the region accesses and never stores; a misaligned base runs a clone of
the region whose accesses keep their tests:

    entry:
      or    t, base, other
      andi  t, t, size-1
      bnez  t, slow
      region             ; bases aligned: KnownAlignmentLog2 proves their accesses, no tests
  slow:                  ; rarely run, out of line
    clone of region      ; every access keeps its test, exits go where the region's do

A block joins the region when all its predecessors are in it, it ends in a jump or a conditional jump,
it is in no EH region and not rarely run, its nodes are ones perf-80 clones (plus the conditional jump)
and it stores neither the base nor the struct it is a field of. So the region is entered only through
the test (a jump back to the entry tests again) and the bases keep their tested values throughout.
Blocks that lead to no access through the bases are left out, and another base is covered only when its
accesses run more often than the entry. Of all regions, the one that saves the most instructions,
weighted by block weight, is versioned; up to 16 per method, within the clone budget perf-80 already
has. The region's blocks record the tested alignment as facts, which perf-80's runs then build on; the
clones keep the facts of their originals.

Nethermind stateless guest on SP1 (sp1-runner v6.6.0), on top of perf-76..80, cycles:
  block 26055159:  96,594,868 ->  96,097,609 (-0.51%), gas -0.20%
  block 26112793: 210,570,273 -> 209,036,962 (-0.73%), gas -0.33%
  block 11940631 (Glamsterdam): 611,675,441 -> 607,594,579 (-0.67%), gas -0.57%
  outputs identical. On 26112793 the alignment tests fall from 5.43% to 4.67% of cycles, and tests that
  repeat one made earlier in the function from 1.29M to 0.36M; AddMod and ReduceSumAssumingLT2m keep 4 of
  their 16 tests and cost 18 cycles less per ADDMOD. The SP1 guest ELF grows by 140 KB (+1.6%).
Only builds with JitRiscV64StrictAlign change: the ZisK guest builds byte-identical.
Regression test: tools/tests/StrictAlignment/FastPath.

